 ## Overview
-Voxel Ray Traversal (AABB-optimized) is an iterator-based algorithm that traverses a 3D voxel volume along a ray, returning hits for voxels contained within the volume [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently enumerating occupied voxels in sparse volumes by combining a standard 3D DDA (Digital Differential Analyzer) traversal with an initial Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) rejection test [S1, unofficial]. What sets it apart from its siblings, such as [Octree Traversal](1587-octree-traversal.md) or [Sparse Voxel Ray Marching with AABB Exit Planes](3252-sparse-voxel-ray-marching-with-aabb-exit-planes.md), is its reliance on a flat or chunk-based volume structure where the AABB test serves primarily as a "jump ahead" mechanism to skip empty space before the voxel-by-voxel iteration begins, rather than using hierarchical node structures for traversal [S1, unofficial].
